// Fresh Question Bank - Domain 5.0 Security Program Management and Oversight
window.FRESH_BANK = (window.FRESH_BANK || []).concat([
// ---------- 5.1 Governance (24) ----------
{o:"5.1",q:"A document approved by the board states: 'All company data must be protected according to its classification.' It names no technologies and gives no steps. Which type of governance document is it?",c:["Technical standard requiring specific controls","Security policy stating management intent","Operating procedure for staff to follow","Guideline offering optional recommendations"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A standard sets specific mandatory technical requirements, such as algorithms or settings. This statement names none, so it sits above the standard level. It would be a standard if it said 'use AES-256'.","Correct. A policy is a high-level, mandatory statement of management intent. It says what must be achieved (protect data by classification) without prescribing technologies or steps, which are left to standards and procedures.","Incorrect. A procedure lists ordered steps for performing a task. This text contains no steps. It would be a procedure if it walked staff through how to label and handle each class of data.","Incorrect. A guideline is advisory and not enforceable. The wording 'must' makes this mandatory. It would be a guideline if it said staff 'should consider' classifying data."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"A document states: 'All laptops must use AES-256 full-disk encryption and passwords of at least 14 characters.' It does not explain how to enable either setting. Which type of document is it?",c:["Policy stating management intent","Procedure describing the steps to follow","Guideline with optional recommendations","Standard defining mandatory settings"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A policy is broader and names no algorithms or lengths. The specific values here put the text below policy level. It would be a policy if it said only that laptop data must be protected.","Incorrect. A procedure gives ordered how-to steps, and the stem says no steps are given. It would be a procedure if it described how to turn on BitLocker or FileVault.","Incorrect. Guidelines are optional advice. The word 'must' makes these requirements mandatory. It would be a guideline if the text said encryption is 'recommended'.","Correct. A standard defines specific, mandatory technical requirements (AES-256, 14 characters) that support a higher-level policy. It specifies what to configure without explaining the steps to do it."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"The help desk lead writes a numbered checklist that technicians follow to verify a caller's identity before a password reset, including the exact questions to ask. Which type of document is this?",c:["Procedure listing the required steps","Policy setting management expectations","Standard specifying required settings","Guideline suggesting good practices"],a:0,e:["Correct. A procedure is a detailed, ordered set of instructions for carrying out a task consistently. The numbered checklist with exact questions is the classic how-to level of documentation.","Incorrect. A policy states high-level intent, such as 'identities must be verified before resets'. It does not list questions or steps. This text is too detailed to be a policy.","Incorrect. A standard defines mandatory technical values, such as minimum password length. It does not walk a technician through a call. It would fit if the text set the required verification factors.","Incorrect. A guideline is optional advice. The checklist must be followed exactly. It would be a guideline if technicians were merely encouraged to verify callers in whatever way they prefer."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"The security team publishes advice that departments 'should consider' using a password manager, leaving the decision to each manager. Compliance with the advice is not audited. Which type of document is this?",c:["Standard requiring a specific product","Procedure for enrolling in the vault","Guideline with recommended practice","Policy mandating credential protection"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A standard is mandatory and names specific technologies or settings. Nothing here is required or audited. It would be a standard if it said every department must deploy a named product.","Incorrect. A procedure gives ordered steps for a task. This text gives no steps and only suggests an idea. It would be a procedure if it explained how to enroll in the vault.","Correct. Guidelines are non-mandatory recommendations that leave flexibility to the reader. 'Should consider' and the lack of auditing signal advisory material.","Incorrect. A policy is mandatory management direction, and compliance is enforced. The optional wording here rules it out. It would be a policy if it said credentials must be protected."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"The VP of Finance is accountable for the payroll data set, decides its classification level, and approves who may be granted access. Which governance role is she filling?",c:["Data custodian for the payroll system","Data steward for the payroll system","Data processor for the payroll vendor","Data owner for the payroll system"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A custodian carries out technical protection, such as backups and permissions, on the owner's instructions. The VP is making the decisions. It would fit an administrator implementing them.","Incorrect. A steward manages data quality, definitions and metadata. The VP is setting classification and access, which are owner decisions. It would fit someone maintaining payroll data standards.","Incorrect. A processor handles data on behalf of a controller, usually an external party. The VP is an internal decision-maker. It would fit the outside company that runs payroll calculations.","Correct. The data owner is a senior business leader accountable for the data, who sets classification and approves access. Custodians and stewards act on those decisions."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"A database administrator runs nightly backups, enables encryption at rest and configures access permissions on the HR database exactly as the HR director specified. She does not decide who gets access or define the data. Which role is she?",c:["Data owner for the HR database","Data steward for the HR database","Data controller for the HR database","Data custodian for the HR database"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. The owner is accountable and decides classification and access, and that decision belongs to the HR director here. It would fit someone approving access requests.","Incorrect. A steward maintains data quality, labeling and definitions. The DBA's work is technical protection. It would fit someone responsible for consistent field definitions.","Incorrect. A controller determines the purposes and means of processing personal data, which is a legal role under GDPR. Following the director's instructions does not make her one.","Correct. The custodian handles day-to-day technical protection of the data (backups, encryption, permissions) according to the owner's decisions."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"Under GDPR, a retailer decides why customer purchase data is collected and which analytics platform will be used to process it. What is the retailer's role?",c:["Data processor of the data set","Data custodian of the data set","Data controller of the data set","Data steward of the data set"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A processor acts only on the controller's instructions. Here the retailer is making the decisions. It would be the analytics vendor running the processing.","Incorrect. Custodian is an internal technical role, not a GDPR legal role. It would fit a staff member administering the retailer's storage.","Correct. The controller determines the purposes and means of processing personal data and carries primary compliance responsibility. Deciding why and how data is processed is the defining test.","Incorrect. A steward manages data quality and definitions internally. It does not decide the purposes of processing. It would fit the employee who maintains customer record standards."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"A payroll company calculates and pays wages for client businesses strictly on their written instructions and has no right to use the data for its own purposes. Under GDPR, what is the payroll company?",c:["Data controller for the client employees","Data processor for the client employees","Data owner for the client employees","Data steward for the client employees"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. The controller decides the purposes and means. The clients make those decisions here. The payroll company would be a controller only if it chose why and how to use the data.","Correct. A processor handles personal data on behalf of a controller and only on documented instructions. The payroll firm follows its clients' directions and has no independent purpose.","Incorrect. Owner is an internal governance role for a senior business leader. Each client's own leaders would hold it. It is not a GDPR relationship between two organizations.","Incorrect. A steward maintains quality and metadata inside an organization. It does not describe a vendor processing data. It would fit an in-house person maintaining payroll data definitions."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"A company assigns a staff member to maintain data definitions, metadata and quality rules, and to make sure records are labeled consistently across departments. She does not administer servers or approve access. Which role is this?",c:["Data steward for the business unit","Data custodian for the business unit","Data owner for the business unit","Data controller for the business unit"],a:0,e:["Correct. A data steward is responsible for data quality, metadata and proper labeling and use. The lack of server administration or access approval separates the role from custodian and owner.","Incorrect. A custodian performs technical protection, such as storage, backup and permissions. The stem excludes server administration. It would fit the person running backups.","Incorrect. The owner is accountable for the data and approves access and classification. The stem says she does not approve access. It would fit the senior leader responsible for the data set.","Incorrect. A controller is a GDPR legal role that decides the purposes of processing. Maintaining metadata is not that decision. It would fit the entity choosing why data is collected."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"Executives from IT, legal, finance and operations meet monthly to set security direction, approve major policies and review enterprise risk. Which body does this describe?",c:["Change advisory board for system changes","Security operations center for alerts","Executive security governance committee","Incident response team for breaches"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A CAB reviews and approves individual technical changes. These executives are setting direction and approving policy. It would fit a group evaluating a firewall change request.","Incorrect. A SOC monitors and triages security events day to day. It does not set organization-wide policy. It would fit a team watching a SIEM.","Correct. A governance board or committee of senior stakeholders provides oversight, sets direction and approves major policies. Cross-functional executive membership and policy approval are the clues.","Incorrect. An incident response team acts when a security event occurs. It does not meet monthly to approve policy. It would fit a group handling an active breach."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"Each regional business unit selects its own security tools and sets its own access rules, with corporate providing only general guidance. Which governance structure is this?",c:["Centralized with one security office","Decentralized with unit-level choices","Committee-based with executive approval","Matrix with dual reporting lines"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Centralized governance keeps decisions at the top, with one team setting rules for all. Here units decide for themselves. It would fit a single security office mandating one tool set.","Correct. In decentralized governance, business units make many of their own security decisions, which gives flexibility but can produce inconsistent controls.","Incorrect. A committee model has an executive body that approves policy for the whole organization. The stem shows units acting independently. It would fit a board that must sign off before tools are chosen.","Incorrect. Matrix describes reporting lines, with staff reporting to two managers. It does not say who makes security decisions. It would fit a staffing question."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"A publicly traded manufacturer must prove to auditors that the IT systems feeding its financial statements are protected against unauthorized changes. Which law drives this requirement?",c:["HIPAA covering patient health records","FERPA covering student education records","GLBA covering consumer financial privacy","SOX covering financial report integrity"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. HIPAA protects health information held by covered entities. A manufacturer's financial systems are outside its scope. It would apply to a clinic or health plan.","Incorrect. FERPA protects student education records at schools that receive federal funds. It would apply to a university registrar system.","Incorrect. GLBA requires financial institutions to protect customers' nonpublic personal information. A manufacturer is not a financial institution. It would apply to a bank or lender.","Correct. Sarbanes-Oxley requires public companies to maintain and attest to internal controls over financial reporting, which includes the IT general controls behind those statements."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"A clinic's outside billing vendor stores patient treatment records and must sign a business associate agreement. Which US law requires this arrangement?",c:["HIPAA rules for health records","SOX financial reporting controls","FISMA federal agency system security","GLBA financial privacy rules"],a:0,e:["Correct. HIPAA requires covered entities to have business associate agreements with vendors that handle protected health information, extending safeguards to them.","Incorrect. SOX concerns financial reporting controls at public companies. Patient records are not its subject. It would apply to a listed company's accounting systems.","Incorrect. FISMA governs security programs for federal agency systems. A private clinic's vendor is not in scope. It would apply to a federal agency or its contractors.","Incorrect. GLBA applies to financial institutions handling consumer financial data. Treatment records are health data. It would apply to a bank's customer information."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"A federal agency's procurement rules require that VPN appliances use cryptographic modules validated under a US government standard. Which should the vendor provide?",c:["ISO/IEC 27001 certification","FIPS 140-3 validation certificate","PCI DSS attestation of compliance","SOC 2 Type II examination report"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. ISO/IEC 27001 certifies an organization's ISMS, not individual cryptographic modules. It would fit a request for proof of a management system.","Correct. FIPS 140-3 defines security requirements for cryptographic modules, and federal agencies require validated modules. The validation certificate is the proof.","Incorrect. A PCI DSS attestation covers protection of cardholder data environments. It says nothing about module validation. It would fit a payment processor.","Incorrect. A SOC 2 Type II report describes how a service provider's controls operated over time. It does not validate cryptographic modules. It would fit a cloud service assessment."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"A merchant accepts Visa and Mastercard payments. Which set of requirements is enforced through contracts with the acquirer and card brands rather than by statute?",c:["HIPAA Security Rule requirements","SOX Section 404 requirements","PCI DSS requirements","GDPR Article 32 requirements"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. The HIPAA Security Rule is a federal regulation covering electronic health information. It would apply if the merchant were a healthcare provider.","Incorrect. SOX Section 404 is a statute requiring internal controls over financial reporting at public companies. It is law, not a contractual standard.","Correct. PCI DSS is an industry standard created by the card brands and enforced contractually, with fines or loss of card processing privileges as penalties.","Incorrect. GDPR Article 32 is EU law requiring appropriate security of personal data. It would apply to processing EU residents' data. It is a statute, not a contractual standard."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"An organization wants an external auditor to issue a certificate showing its information security management system meets an international standard. Which should it implement?",c:["ISO/IEC 27002 control guidance","ISO/IEC 27001 ISMS requirements","ISO 31000 risk management guidelines","NIST SP 800-53 control catalog"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. ISO/IEC 27002 gives implementation guidance for controls, but organizations cannot be certified to it. It supports 27001 rather than replacing it.","Correct. ISO/IEC 27001 specifies ISMS requirements and is the standard against which accredited auditors certify an organization.","Incorrect. ISO 31000 offers risk management principles and guidelines and is not a certification standard. It would suit a question about enterprise risk process design.","Incorrect. NIST SP 800-53 is a catalog of security and privacy controls, mainly for US federal systems. It has no accredited certificate. It would fit a federal compliance program."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"An organization adopts a framework that groups cybersecurity outcomes into six functions: Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond and Recover. Which framework is this?",c:["NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0","ISO/IEC 27001 Annex A control set","CIS Critical Security Controls v8","NIST Risk Management Framework"],a:0,e:["Correct. CSF 2.0 organizes outcomes into six functions. Govern was added in version 2.0 alongside the original five.","Incorrect. ISO/IEC 27001 Annex A groups controls into themes such as organizational, people, physical and technological. It has no function model like this.","Incorrect. CIS Controls v8 is a prioritized list of 18 controls with implementation groups, not six functions. It would fit a question about prioritizing safeguards.","Incorrect. The NIST RMF is a seven-step lifecycle (Prepare, Categorize, Select, Implement, Assess, Authorize, Monitor). It would fit a question on authorizing a federal system."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"A new policy tells employees that company laptops may not be used for personal cryptocurrency mining or gambling sites, and that use is monitored. Which policy is this?",c:["Change management policy for IT systems","Business continuity plan for disruptions","Incident response plan for security events","Acceptable use policy for company assets"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A change management policy governs how modifications to systems are requested, approved and tested. It would fit a rule requiring approval before patching production.","Incorrect. A business continuity plan keeps critical functions running during a disruption. It says nothing about personal use of devices.","Incorrect. An incident response plan defines how to detect and handle security events. It would fit a document describing escalation and containment steps.","Correct. The AUP states what users may and may not do with company IT resources and warns that monitoring occurs, which is exactly the content described."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"Auditors need chronological evidence showing which account performed each privileged action on the finance system so that compliance with procedures can be proven. Which record meets this need?",c:["Application debug logs kept for developers","Vulnerability scan reports from last quarter","Protected audit trails of user actions","Backup job logs for the finance database"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Debug logs help developers troubleshoot code and are often verbose, unprotected and not tied to accountability. They would fit diagnosing an application crash.","Incorrect. Scan reports list weaknesses in systems, not who did what. They would fit evidence that vulnerability management is performed.","Correct. Audit trails record who performed which action and when, in a protected form that serves as proof for auditors.","Incorrect. Backup job logs show whether backups ran. They would fit evidence for a backup policy but do not attribute privileged actions to accounts."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"Which task is NOT normally part of a documented cryptographic key management lifecycle?",c:["Generating keys inside an HSM","Rotating keys on a defined schedule","Destroying keys at end of life","Upgrading the app's cipher library"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Generation, ideally inside a hardware security module, is the first lifecycle stage, so it is part of key management.","Incorrect. Rotation limits the exposure of any single key and is a standard lifecycle activity.","Incorrect. Secure destruction at end of life is the final lifecycle stage and prevents later recovery of the key.","Correct. Upgrading a cipher library is software maintenance. It may affect which algorithms are available, but it is not a stage in the life of a key."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"A bank must keep customer transaction records for at least seven years because a statute says so, and may not shorten the period to save storage costs even though peers do. Which external consideration drives this retention period?",c:["Statutory record-retention laws","Industry peer-group benchmarks","Cyber insurance policy conditions","Internal storage cost reduction goals"],a:0,e:["Correct. Laws and regulations set mandatory retention periods, which override cost or convenience. The statutory requirement is the clue.","Incorrect. Peer practice can inform decisions but is not binding, and the stem says the bank cannot follow peers who shorten retention. It would fit a question on voluntary benchmarks.","Incorrect. Insurers may impose security requirements, but they do not set statutory retention minimums. It would apply if the stem cited a policy condition.","Incorrect. Internal cost targets are an internal consideration, and the statute overrides them. It would apply where no law dictates retention."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"After a regional power outage, executives want a plan that keeps order processing, customer support and logistics running at alternate sites, covering staff and facilities as well as IT. Which plan is this?",c:["Disaster recovery plan for IT systems","Business continuity plan for all functions","Incident response plan for security events","Business impact analysis for critical processes"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A DRP focuses on restoring IT systems and data. The stem includes staff, facilities and business functions beyond IT. It would fit a plan to rebuild servers.","Correct. A business continuity plan covers keeping all critical business functions operating during and after a disruption, including people, sites and processes.","Incorrect. An incident response plan handles detection and containment of security events. A power outage is not a security incident process. It would fit a malware outbreak.","Incorrect. A BIA identifies critical functions and quantifies impact to inform the plans. It does not itself keep operations running. It would fit work that precedes the BCP."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"After a data center fire, the IT director wants a document that details the order for restoring servers, databases and network services at the hot site. It does not cover staff relocation or customer communications. Which plan is this?",c:["Disaster recovery plan (DRP)","Business continuity plan (BCP)","Incident response plan (IRP)","Business impact analysis (BIA)"],a:0,e:["Correct. A DRP is the IT-focused plan for restoring systems and data after a disaster, including recovery order and alternate-site procedures.","Incorrect. A BCP covers the whole business, including people and facilities. The stem excludes those. It would fit a plan to relocate staff and keep customer service running.","Incorrect. An IR plan covers handling a security incident, such as containment and eradication. A fire is not a security incident. It would fit a ransomware event.","Incorrect. A BIA analyzes impact and sets recovery targets. It does not contain the restoration steps. It would fit the study that sets the RTO for each system."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"A company is writing a secure software development lifecycle policy. Which approach matches its intent?",c:["Run one penetration test after the production release","Hold a single manual code review before the final build","Add a security gate to each development phase","Scan for vulnerabilities after deployment to production"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A post-release penetration test finds problems only after they are in production, where fixes cost the most. It is a useful supplement but not the intent of an SDLC policy.","Incorrect. A single review late in development catches some coding flaws but misses requirement and design weaknesses. It would be a partial control, not lifecycle-wide security.","Correct. A secure SDLC builds security into requirements, design, coding, testing and deployment so defects are found early and cheaply.","Incorrect. Scanning in production is a detective control in operations. It does not shift security left into development. It would fit ongoing vulnerability management."]},
// ---------- 5.2 Risk management (28) ----------
{o:"5.2",q:"A ransomware outage on an e-commerce platform is estimated to cost $25,000 per event (SLE) and is expected about once every 20 years. What is the ALE?",c:["Annualized loss of $5,000","Annualized loss of $12,500","Annualized loss of $1,250","Annualized loss of $500,000"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. $5,000 would result from an ARO of 0.2, meaning one event every 5 years. The stem says every 20 years, so the ARO is 0.05.","Incorrect. $12,500 would result from an ARO of 0.5, one event every 2 years. This misreads the frequency by a factor of ten.","Correct. ARO = 1/20 = 0.05, so ALE = SLE x ARO = $25,000 x 0.05 = $1,250 per year.","Incorrect. $500,000 comes from multiplying the SLE by 20 instead of by 1/20. The ARO is a fraction of an occurrence per year, so the ALE must be smaller than the SLE."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A server is valued at $80,000. A flood would destroy 25% of its value. What is the single loss expectancy (SLE)?",c:["Single loss expectancy of $20,000","Single loss expectancy of $60,000","Single loss expectancy of $320,000","Single loss expectancy of $100,000"],a:0,e:["Correct. SLE = asset value x exposure factor = $80,000 x 0.25 = $20,000.","Incorrect. $60,000 is the value that would remain after the loss (75%), not the loss itself. It would be the answer only if the flood destroyed 75%.","Incorrect. $320,000 multiplies the asset value by 4, dividing by 0.25 instead of multiplying. The loss cannot exceed the asset's value at an EF below 100%.","Incorrect. $100,000 adds the loss to the asset value, which has no meaning in the SLE formula."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A flood that causes $20,000 in damage each time it occurs is expected once every 5 years. What is the ALE?",c:["Annualized loss of $20,000","Annualized loss of $4,000","Annualized loss of $5,000","Annualized loss of $100,000"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. $20,000 is the SLE. It would equal the ALE only if the event were expected once per year (ARO = 1).","Correct. ARO = 1/5 = 0.2, so ALE = $20,000 x 0.2 = $4,000 per year.","Incorrect. $5,000 would come from an ARO of 0.25, which implies one event every 4 years rather than 5.","Incorrect. $100,000 multiplies by 5 instead of dividing. An event expected less than once a year has an ALE smaller than its SLE."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"An analyst states that a fire would destroy 40% of a warehouse's inventory value. Which risk term does the 40% represent?",c:["Single loss expectancy (SLE)","Annualized rate of occurrence (ARO)","Annualized loss expectancy (ALE)","Exposure factor (EF)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. SLE is the dollar loss from one event, calculated as asset value x EF. The 40% is an input to the SLE, not the SLE itself.","Incorrect. ARO is how many times per year an event is expected, such as 0.1. A percentage of value is not a frequency.","Incorrect. ALE is the expected dollar loss per year, equal to SLE x ARO. It is a dollar figure, not a percentage of value.","Correct. The exposure factor is the percentage of an asset's value lost in a single incident."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A risk workshop plots each risk on a heat map with likelihood rated High and impact rated Medium. No dollar values are assigned. Which type of analysis is this?",c:["Qualitative analysis of the risks","Vulnerability analysis of assets","Quantitative analysis using ALE","Business impact analysis of processes"],a:0,e:["Correct. Qualitative analysis uses ranked categories and judgment rather than monetary values, and heat maps are a typical output.","Incorrect. Vulnerability analysis finds technical weaknesses in assets. It does not rate the likelihood and impact of risk events on a heat map.","Incorrect. Quantitative analysis assigns numbers such as SLE, ARO and ALE. The stem uses ratings only. It would fit a worksheet with dollar loss calculations.","Incorrect. A BIA focuses on how disruption of business functions affects the organization and sets recovery targets. A risk heat map is not its output."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"Historical failure data shows a 7% chance that a given hard drive fails this year. Which term describes this measurement?",c:["Exposure factor as a percentage","Impact as a dollar-value loss","Probability as a numeric value","Likelihood as a descriptive rating"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Exposure factor is the share of asset value lost when an event occurs. The 7% is the chance of the event occurring.","Incorrect. Impact measures the consequence if the drive fails. The 7% describes chance, not consequence.","Correct. Probability is the numeric measure of how likely an event is, here 7%, and is used in quantitative analysis.","Incorrect. Likelihood is the qualitative version, expressed as categories like low, medium or high. The stem gives a number, not a category."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A workshop rates each risk as rare, unlikely, possible, likely or almost certain. Which measure is being assigned to each risk?",c:["Probability value of each event","Annualized rate of occurrence","Single loss expectancy per event","Likelihood level of each event"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Probability is a numeric value or percentage. It would fit if the workshop assigned figures such as 5% or 40%.","Incorrect. ARO is a number of expected occurrences per year, for example 0.1. Descriptive labels do not give an ARO.","Incorrect. SLE is a dollar amount lost in one event. The labels measure chance, not money.","Correct. Likelihood is the qualitative measure of how probable a risk event is over a period, expressed in descriptive categories."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"After a threat assessment, leadership cancels the planned launch of an online gambling product in a new country, so the exposure no longer exists. Which risk response was chosen?",c:["Transfer as the chosen response","Avoidance as the chosen response","Mitigation as the chosen response","Acceptance as the chosen response"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Transfer shifts the financial impact to a third party, for example through insurance, while the activity continues.","Correct. Avoidance eliminates the risk by stopping the activity that creates it. Cancelling the launch removes the exposure.","Incorrect. Mitigation adds controls to reduce likelihood or impact while the activity continues. It would fit launching with extra fraud controls.","Incorrect. Acceptance means knowingly living with the risk. Cancelling the project does not do that. It would fit launching with a documented sign-off."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A company deploys a web application firewall in front of its customer portal to reduce the number of successful injection attacks. The portal stays online. Which risk response is this?",c:["Avoidance as the chosen response","Mitigation as the chosen response","Transfer as the chosen response","Acceptance as the chosen response"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Avoidance would end the activity, such as taking the portal offline. The portal continues operating here.","Correct. Mitigation applies controls, here a WAF, to reduce the likelihood or impact of a risk while the activity continues.","Incorrect. Transfer moves the financial burden to someone else, for example through cyber insurance or a contractual indemnity. A WAF does not shift liability.","Incorrect. Acceptance would mean taking no action beyond documenting the risk. Installing a WAF is an active treatment."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A retailer buys a cyber liability policy that pays breach response and legal costs above a deductible. Its security controls and operations stay as they are. Which risk response is this?",c:["Avoidance as the chosen response","Mitigation as the chosen response","Transfer as the chosen response","Acceptance as the chosen response"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Avoidance means ceasing the risky activity. The retailer continues to operate and hold customer data.","Incorrect. Mitigation reduces likelihood or impact through controls. The stem says controls and operations are unchanged, so the probability of a breach stays the same.","Correct. Insurance shifts part of the financial impact to a third party. Contracts, outsourcing and indemnification are other forms of transfer.","Incorrect. Acceptance means absorbing the full loss itself. Here a share of the loss is covered by the insurer. It would fit a decision to self-insure."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"Management reviews a low-rated risk, determines remediation would cost more than the potential loss, and signs a documented decision to live with it. Which risk response is this?",c:["Avoidance as the chosen response","Mitigation as the chosen response","Transfer as the chosen response","Acceptance as the chosen response"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Avoidance would stop the activity creating the risk. The business keeps operating unchanged.","Incorrect. Mitigation would add controls. The cost-benefit result here is to add none.","Incorrect. Transfer would involve insurance or contracts to shift the loss. Nothing is shifted here.","Correct. Acceptance is a legitimate response when treatment costs more than the expected loss, provided it is a documented decision by the right authority."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"The board issues a broad, strategy-level statement describing how much risk the organization is willing to pursue to meet its goals. It is not tied to one metric. What is this?",c:["Risk threshold of the organization","Risk tolerance of the organization","Risk appetite of the organization","Residual risk of the organization"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A threshold is a specific trigger point for action, such as a dollar figure that forces escalation. The board statement here is not a trigger.","Incorrect. Tolerance is the acceptable deviation around a specific objective or metric. It would fit a statement allowing 5% variance in uptime.","Correct. Risk appetite is the broad, strategic amount and type of risk an organization will pursue to achieve its objectives.","Incorrect. Residual risk is what remains after controls are applied to a particular risk. It is an assessed value, not a strategic stance."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"An investment firm allows monthly portfolio returns to deviate up to 10% from target. Beyond that, immediate corrective action is required. What does the 10% allowance represent?",c:["Risk tolerance for the portfolio","Risk appetite for the portfolio","Inherent risk for the portfolio","Risk register entry for the portfolio"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Appetite is the broad strategic stance on risk. A numeric deviation allowance around a specific objective is narrower than that.","Correct. Risk tolerance is the acceptable variation around an objective. Crossing it triggers action.","Incorrect. Inherent risk is the level of risk before any controls are applied. The allowance is not an assessment of a particular risk.","Incorrect. A register entry records an identified risk with its owner and status. It is a tracking record, not an allowed deviation."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"Policy states that any vulnerability with a potential loss over $250,000 must be escalated to the CISO within 24 hours. What is the $250,000 figure?",c:["Key risk indicator for vulnerabilities","Risk threshold for vulnerabilities","Risk appetite for vulnerabilities","Residual risk of vulnerabilities"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A KRI is a metric monitored for early warning, such as failed logins per week. The figure here is a cutoff, not a monitored measure.","Correct. A threshold is a defined point that triggers a specific response. Here it triggers escalation.","Incorrect. Appetite is a broad strategic position, not a dollar line that triggers a procedure.","Incorrect. Residual risk is the level remaining after controls for a specific risk. The $250,000 is a policy cutoff that applies across risks."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"After MFA and EDR are deployed on the payroll system, the team records the level of risk that remains. What is this recorded value?",c:["Residual risk of the payroll system","Risk threshold of the payroll system","Risk tolerance of the payroll system","Inherent risk of the payroll system"],a:0,e:["Correct. Residual risk is the risk that remains after controls are applied.","Incorrect. A threshold is a trigger point for action. Comparing residual risk to it may prompt escalation, but it is not the remaining risk itself.","Incorrect. Tolerance is the acceptable level of deviation, a target that residual risk is compared against, not the measured remainder.","Incorrect. Inherent risk is the level before any controls. It would be recorded as the starting point before MFA and EDR were deployed."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A living document records every identified risk along with its owner, likelihood, impact, status and planned response, and is updated as work proceeds. What is it?",c:["Risk report for the executive team","Business impact analysis (BIA)","Vulnerability assessment report","Enterprise risk register"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A risk report summarizes status and trends for stakeholders at a point in time. It is drawn from the register, not the working record itself.","Incorrect. A BIA analyzes the impact of losing business functions and sets recovery targets. It does not track owners and responses for each risk.","Incorrect. A vulnerability assessment report lists technical weaknesses found in systems. It does not record risk ownership and responses.","Correct. The risk register is the central, continuously updated tracking tool for identified risks and their treatment."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"IT leadership tracks failed administrator logins per week because a rising trend has preceded past breaches. What kind of metric is this?",c:["Residual risk after controls","Annualized rate of occurrence","Risk threshold for escalation","Key risk indicator (KRI)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Residual risk is the level remaining after controls. The login count is an observation that may signal rising risk.","Incorrect. ARO is an estimated number of loss events per year used in calculations. A weekly count of failed logins is not a loss-event estimate.","Incorrect. A threshold is the cutoff at which action is triggered. A KRI may be compared to one, but the weekly count itself is the indicator.","Correct. KRIs are monitored metrics that give early warning that risk exposure is increasing."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"Each identified risk needs one person who is accountable for monitoring it and approving how it will be treated. Who should be assigned?",c:["SOC analyst monitoring alerts","System custodian administering servers","Business unit leader as risk owner","Internal auditor reviewing controls"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. An analyst monitors and triages events and does not hold authority to approve treatment of a risk. It fits detection work.","Incorrect. A custodian carries out technical protection tasks. It does not hold accountability for the risk decision. It fits implementing a chosen control.","Correct. A risk owner has accountability and authority to manage and approve treatment of a risk, typically a business or functional leader.","Incorrect. Auditors assess independently and must not own the risks they evaluate. An auditor would be right for verifying that treatment is effective."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"Before choosing which systems to restore first after a disaster, leadership needs a study that identifies critical business functions and the impact of losing them over time. What is this study?",c:["Disaster recovery plan (DRP)","Business impact analysis (BIA)","Tabletop exercise for the plan","Vulnerability assessment of systems"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A DRP documents how to restore IT services. It relies on the BIA's priorities rather than producing them.","Correct. A BIA identifies critical functions, quantifies impact of disruption over time and sets recovery priorities and targets such as RTO and RPO.","Incorrect. A tabletop is a discussion-based exercise that tests a plan. It would fit validating the plan after the BIA is done.","Incorrect. A vulnerability assessment finds technical weaknesses. It does not rank business functions by impact of loss."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"The business sets a target that the order system must be back in service within 4 hours of an outage, or contractual penalties begin. Which metric is the 4 hours?",c:["Recovery time objective (RTO)","Mean time to repair (MTTR)","Recovery point objective (RPO)","Mean time between failures (MTBF)"],a:0,e:["Correct. RTO is the maximum acceptable time to restore a process after disruption, a target set by the business.","Incorrect. MTTR is a measured average of how long repairs actually take. The 4 hours here is a target set in advance, not a measurement.","Incorrect. RPO is the maximum acceptable data loss measured in time, for example 15 minutes of transactions. It drives backup frequency, not restoration time.","Incorrect. MTBF measures average operating time between failures and indicates reliability. It says nothing about how quickly service must return."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A payment platform's design must ensure that at most 15 minutes of committed transactions can be lost if the primary site fails. Which metric is this requirement?",c:["Recovery time objective (RTO)","Annualized loss expectancy (ALE)","Mean time to repair (MTTR)","Recovery point objective (RPO)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. RTO concerns how long the service may be down. The requirement here concerns how much data may be lost.","Incorrect. ALE is an expected annual dollar loss from a risk. It is not a recovery metric.","Incorrect. MTTR is the average time to repair a failed component. It does not define acceptable data loss.","Correct. RPO is the maximum tolerable data loss measured in time, and it sets the replication or backup interval."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A storage vendor states that its drive model runs an average of 1.2 million hours between failures. Which metric is this?",c:["Annualized rate of occurrence (ARO)","Mean time between failures (MTBF)","Mean time to repair (MTTR)","Recovery time objective (RTO)"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. ARO is how often an event is expected to happen per year in risk calculations. It is an estimate for a risk scenario, not a vendor reliability statistic.","Correct. MTBF is the average operating time between failures and is used to describe the reliability of a component.","Incorrect. MTTR is how long a repair takes once a failure occurs. It would fit a figure like 4 hours to replace a drive.","Incorrect. RTO is a business target for restoring service. A vendor reliability figure is not a recovery target."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"Over the past year, the network team measured the average time from a switch failing to being back in service as 4 hours. Which metric is this?",c:["Mean time to repair (MTTR)","Mean time between failures (MTBF)","Recovery point objective (RPO)","Recovery time objective (RTO)"],a:0,e:["Correct. MTTR is the measured average time needed to repair a failed component and restore it to service.","Incorrect. MTBF measures the average time a device operates before it fails. The figure here measures the repair duration.","Incorrect. RPO relates to acceptable data loss, which is not described here.","Incorrect. RTO is a target set in advance by the business. The 4 hours here was measured from past events. Tying the two together would make MTTR a way to check the RTO."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A new ERP system is assessed for risk once, before go-live, and the results are filed without a scheduled reassessment. What type of risk assessment is this?",c:["A continuous risk assessment","An ad hoc risk assessment","A one-time risk assessment","A periodic recurring risk assessment"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Continuous assessment evaluates risk on an ongoing basis, often with automated tooling. Nothing ongoing is described.","Incorrect. Ad hoc assessments are triggered by an unplanned event, such as a breach at a peer. This one was planned in advance for go-live.","Correct. A one-time assessment evaluates a specific project or moment, such as before deployment, and is not repeated on a schedule.","Incorrect. Recurring assessments are repeated on a fixed schedule, for example annually. The stem says no reassessment is scheduled."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A cloud posture management tool re-evaluates risk every time a configuration changes, with no schedule and no human trigger. Which type of risk assessment is this?",c:["An ad hoc risk assessment","A one-time risk assessment","A continuous risk assessment","A periodic recurring risk assessment"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Ad hoc assessments follow a specific event and are initiated by people. Here an automated change check runs without human trigger.","Incorrect. A one-time assessment evaluates a single project or moment. This tool runs repeatedly.","Correct. Continuous assessment evaluates risk in real time or near real time, usually through automation.","Incorrect. Recurring assessments occur on a calendar, such as quarterly. The stem says no schedule is used."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"Each quarter, Jacob gives executives a document summarizing all current risks, their status and changes in potential impact. It is built from the underlying tracking record. What is it?",c:["Risk register of all risks","Risk report on current status","Business impact analysis (BIA)","Risk assessment of threats"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. The register is the detailed working record that the report draws on. It would fit the underlying list of risks, owners and responses.","Correct. A risk report communicates the current risk position to stakeholders, summarizing information drawn from the register.","Incorrect. A BIA assesses the impact of disruption to business functions. It is not a periodic summary of the risk position.","Incorrect. A risk assessment is the process of identifying and analyzing risks. A summary of status for executives is the outcome of reporting."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A newly hired security manager is building a risk management process from scratch. Which activity must happen FIRST?",c:["Monitor risks and review results","Analyze risks by likelihood and impact","Select treatment options for each risk","Identify risks to critical assets"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Monitoring is ongoing and follows treatment. It would be first only for an existing, already-treated risk portfolio.","Incorrect. Analysis ranks risks that have already been identified. Without a list of risks there is nothing to analyze.","Incorrect. Treatment such as mitigate, transfer or accept comes after risks have been identified and analyzed.","Correct. Risks must be identified before they can be analyzed, treated or monitored. Identification is the first step in the process."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A legacy HVAC controller cannot meet the 14-character password standard. Management approves non-compliance for 12 months while the vendor builds an upgrade, requiring network segmentation in the meantime. What is this approval?",c:["Policy exception with compensating controls","Standard revision lowering the requirement","Risk avoidance by decommissioning the controller","Risk transfer to the vendor by contract"],a:0,e:["Correct. An exception is a documented, time-limited approval to deviate from a standard, usually paired with compensating controls such as segmentation.","Incorrect. A revision would permanently change the standard for everyone. The deviation here is specific to one device and temporary.","Incorrect. Avoidance would remove the controller or the activity. The controller stays in service for 12 months.","Incorrect. Transfer would shift the financial impact to the vendor through a contract or insurance. Nothing in the stem moves liability."]},
// ---------- 5.3 Third-party risk (20) ----------
{o:"5.3",q:"A company already has a contract governing general terms with a contractor. It now needs a document listing the deliverables, timeline, milestones and cost for building one specific mobile app. Which document is this?",c:["Master service agreement (MSA)","Service-level agreement (SLA)","Non-disclosure agreement (NDA)","Statement of work (SOW)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. The MSA already exists and sets the general terms. It would be the right answer if the two parties had no baseline contract yet.","Incorrect. An SLA defines measurable service levels such as uptime and response time for ongoing services. It does not list deliverables and milestones for a build.","Incorrect. An NDA protects confidential information. It does not describe the work or costs. It would fit sharing the app's design with the contractor.","Correct. A SOW defines the scope, deliverables, schedule and cost of a specific project, and typically sits beneath an existing umbrella agreement."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"A cloud provider commits to 99.95% monthly uptime and a 1-hour response to critical tickets, with service credits if either target is missed. Which agreement contains these terms?",c:["Service-level agreement (SLA)","Statement of work (SOW) document","Business partnership agreement (BPA)","Memorandum of understanding (MOU)"],a:0,e:["Correct. An SLA states measurable service levels and the remedies, such as credits, when they are missed.","Incorrect. A SOW defines the scope and deliverables of a project. It does not set uptime targets with credits.","Incorrect. A BPA covers the terms of a business relationship such as profit sharing and responsibilities, not service-level metrics.","Incorrect. An MOU records mutual intent and is generally non-binding, so it would not carry enforceable credits."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"Two companies sign an agreement establishing payment terms, liability limits and IP ownership that will govern every future project between them. Individual projects will be added later. Which agreement is this?",c:["Memorandum of agreement (MOA)","Statement of work (SOW) for a project","Master service agreement (MSA)","Service-level agreement (SLA)"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. An SLA focuses on service performance levels. The stem concerns general commercial terms.","Incorrect. An MOA records agreed responsibilities between parties, often public bodies. It does not usually establish a commercial framework with payment and liability terms.","Correct. An MSA is the umbrella contract establishing general terms, with SOWs added beneath it for each project.","Incorrect. A SOW covers a single project. The agreement here is meant to govern all future work."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"Two organizations document their shared intent to collaborate on a research initiative. Neither wants to create legally enforceable obligations at this stage. Which document fits?",c:["Master service agreement (MSA)","Memorandum of understanding (MOU)","Service-level agreement (SLA)","Statement of work (SOW) for a project"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. An MSA is a binding commercial contract. It would fit if the parties wanted enforceable terms.","Correct. An MOU expresses mutual intent and is typically non-binding, which fits an early-stage collaboration.","Incorrect. A SOW is a binding description of work and deliverables, which the stem rules out.","Incorrect. An SLA is binding and sets service levels. The parties here have no service to measure."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"Two state agencies formalize each party's specific responsibilities in a document that is more binding than a simple statement of intent, though it is not a commercial contract. Which document is this?",c:["Master service agreement (MSA)","Memorandum of understanding (MOU)","Memorandum of agreement (MOA)","Business partnership agreement (BPA)"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. An MSA is a commercial contract establishing general terms for services. It does not suit an arrangement between agencies.","Incorrect. An MOU expresses intent and is generally non-binding. The stem requires something stronger.","Correct. An MOA spells out agreed responsibilities and carries more weight than an MOU, without being a commercial contract.","Incorrect. A BPA sets commercial partnership terms such as profit sharing. Government agencies defining responsibilities is not that."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"Two companies form a joint venture and need a document covering profit sharing, IP ownership, decision rights and exit terms. Which agreement should they sign?",c:["Business partnership agreement (BPA)","Statement of work (SOW) for a project","Non-disclosure agreement (NDA)","Service-level agreement (SLA)"],a:0,e:["Correct. A BPA sets out the terms of a business relationship, including how profits, ownership and exit are handled.","Incorrect. A SOW describes the work for one project. It does not address ownership structure and profit sharing.","Incorrect. An NDA only covers confidentiality. It might be signed in addition, but it does not allocate profit or IP.","Incorrect. An SLA measures service performance. It would fit a provider-customer relationship with uptime targets."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"Before showing unreleased product designs to a prospective manufacturer, a company has the manufacturer sign a document that legally prohibits sharing the designs with anyone else. What is this document?",c:["Service-level agreement (SLA)","Memorandum of understanding (MOU)","Business partnership agreement (BPA)","Non-disclosure agreement (NDA)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. An SLA sets service levels. It does not restrict how shared information is used.","Incorrect. An MOU states mutual intent and is usually non-binding, so it cannot legally prohibit disclosure.","Incorrect. A BPA allocates profit, ownership and responsibilities in a business relationship. Confidentiality is only one possible clause.","Correct. An NDA creates a legally enforceable duty to keep shared information confidential."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"A company wants to penetration test its SaaS vendor's hosted application. The test must define the scope, test window, permitted techniques and emergency contacts. What must be agreed in writing beforehand?",c:["Service-level agreement covering uptime and credits","Rules of engagement with signed authorization","Master service agreement with payment terms","Memorandum of understanding on cooperation goals"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. An SLA covers performance targets. It does not authorize testing or define permitted techniques.","Correct. Rules of engagement set scope, timing, methods and contacts, and signed authorization from the system owner makes the test legal.","Incorrect. An MSA defines general commercial terms. It would only authorize testing if it contained explicit test provisions.","Incorrect. An MOU is a non-binding statement of intent and is not enough to authorize active testing of someone else's system."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"Before signing with a prospective payroll vendor, the security team reviews the vendor's financial stability, reputation, breach history and security practices. Which activity is this?",c:["Supply chain analysis process","Vendor due diligence process","Right-to-audit inspection process","Ongoing vendor monitoring process"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Supply chain analysis examines the vendor's own suppliers and components. This review is about the vendor itself.","Correct. Due diligence is a thorough pre-contract investigation to confirm that a vendor's practices fit the organization's needs.","Incorrect. A right-to-audit inspection takes place under a signed contract clause. The vendor has not signed yet.","Incorrect. Ongoing monitoring happens after onboarding to track a vendor's performance and posture. It would fit annual reviews of a current vendor."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"Contract language lets the customer or its assessors inspect the vendor's security controls and facilities on reasonable notice. What is this provision?",c:["Right-to-audit clause in the contract","Termination for convenience clause","Confidentiality clause in the contract","Indemnification clause in the contract"],a:0,e:["Correct. A right-to-audit clause lets the customer verify that the vendor's controls match its claims.","Incorrect. Termination for convenience lets a party end the contract without cause. It does not provide access to controls.","Incorrect. A confidentiality clause restricts disclosure of information. It does not grant inspection rights.","Incorrect. Indemnification allocates liability for losses. It would fit language making the vendor pay for third-party claims."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"A customer wants evidence that a vendor's security controls operated effectively across the prior 12 months, not merely that they were designed well. Which item best supports this?",c:["Self-completed security questionnaire","SOC 2 Type I examination report","Penetration test summary from last year","SOC 2 Type II examination report"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A questionnaire is self-reported and unverified. It is useful for screening but does not provide independent assurance.","Incorrect. A Type I report evaluates the design of controls at a single point in time. It would fit a request for evidence of design only.","Incorrect. A penetration test shows exploitable weaknesses at one time. It does not demonstrate control operation across 12 months.","Correct. A Type II report is an independent auditor's opinion on whether controls operated effectively over a period of time."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"The IT manager leading a vendor selection realizes the sales representative for one bidder is his brother-in-law. Which type of conflict of interest does this raise?",c:["Vendor lock-in and dependency concern","Competitive relationship conflict","Personal relationship conflict","Financial interest conflict"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Vendor lock-in is a dependency risk after selection, not a conflict affecting the selection decision.","Incorrect. A competitive conflict arises when the vendor is tied to a rival. Nothing suggests that here.","Correct. A family tie can bias the selection. The manager should disclose it and recuse himself.","Incorrect. A financial conflict involves a monetary incentive, such as a commission or ownership stake. None is described."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"An outside consultant advising on vendor selection would earn a percentage of the contract value if a particular vendor is chosen. Which type of conflict of interest is this?",c:["Competitive relationship conflict","Financial interest conflict","Personal relationship conflict","Vendor lock-in and dependency concern"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A competitive conflict involves ties to a rival. No competitor is mentioned.","Correct. A monetary incentive tied to the outcome can bias a recommendation.","Incorrect. A personal conflict involves family or friendship. The stem describes a commission.","Incorrect. Vendor lock-in is a later dependency problem. It does not explain why this advice might be biased."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"A candidate analytics vendor is also under contract with the company's closest competitor and would hold both companies' sales data. Which type of conflict of interest is most relevant?",c:["Competitive relationship conflict","Financial interest conflict","Vendor lock-in and dependency concern","Personal relationship conflict"],a:0,e:["Correct. Serving a competitor raises risks around data separation, prioritization and information leakage.","Incorrect. No commission or ownership stake is described.","Incorrect. Lock-in concerns difficulty switching vendors later, not the vendor's competing allegiances.","Incorrect. Nothing links any employee to the vendor personally."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"A manufacturer maps its suppliers' suppliers to learn where hardware components originate and where tampering could occur before they reach the company. Which activity is this?",c:["Penetration test of suppliers","Right-to-audit inspection","Supply chain analysis","Single-vendor due diligence"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A penetration test probes technical vulnerabilities. It does not reveal where components originate.","Incorrect. A right-to-audit inspection checks one vendor's controls under contract. It does not map components across tiers.","Correct. Supply chain analysis examines multiple tiers of suppliers and the origin of components to find risk, including tampering and counterfeit parts.","Incorrect. Due diligence evaluates one prospective vendor. It does not trace the vendor's own supply base."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"A vendor passed assessment two years ago, then changed hosting providers and reported an incident. The customer never noticed. Which practice would have caught these changes?",c:["Rules of engagement for testing","Right-to-audit clause in the contract","One-time due diligence at onboarding","Ongoing vendor monitoring program"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Rules of engagement govern authorized security testing and do not track a vendor's changes.","Incorrect. A right-to-audit clause permits inspection but does not by itself ensure anyone is checking. Monitoring uses it.","Incorrect. Due diligence is performed before contracting and is a snapshot. It would fit the initial vetting.","Correct. Vendor risk continues after signing, so periodic reassessment and monitoring of posture and changes are needed."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"A customer asks a vendor for its recent internal audit reports. What is the primary goal?",c:["Establish the vendor's regulatory jurisdiction","Compare the vendor's pricing with competitors","Confirm the vendor follows its own controls","Determine the vendor's financial solvency"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Jurisdiction comes from contracts and corporate registration. Audit reports do not establish it.","Incorrect. Pricing comparisons come from proposals and quotes, not audit reports.","Correct. Internal audit evidence shows whether the vendor actually follows its own policies and controls.","Incorrect. Financial stability is assessed through financial statements during due diligence. Internal audit reports address control compliance."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"The facilities team receives a document under an existing MSA that authorizes replacing 20 access points in Building C, with a price and completion date. What is it?",c:["Memorandum of understanding (MOU)","Business partnership agreement (BPA)","Non-disclosure agreement (NDA)","Work order under the MSA"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. An NDA covers confidentiality and does not describe work or price.","Incorrect. An MOU expresses intent and is usually non-binding, so it would not authorize a priced task.","Incorrect. A BPA creates a broad business relationship. It does not authorize a single task.","Correct. A work order or SOW describes one specific task, price and timeline under the existing master agreement."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"A contract requires the vendor to report suspected breaches within 48 hours and meet defined security controls. Why include such terms?",c:["They shift regulatory accountability for the data to the vendor","They make protection and notification duties enforceable","They remove the need for periodic vendor reassessment","They guarantee the vendor will not suffer a breach"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. The customer generally remains accountable to regulators for its data even when a vendor handles it. Contracts can allocate cost, not eliminate accountability.","Correct. Contractual security terms make protection and notification duties enforceable, with remedies if the vendor fails.","Incorrect. A contract cannot prevent a breach. It creates obligations and remedies if one happens.","Incorrect. Contract terms set expectations, but the customer still needs to verify compliance through monitoring and audits."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"During vendor selection, which item is an independent assessment of the vendor's security program?",c:["ISO/IEC 27001 certificate issued by a registrar","Vendor-completed security questionnaire with evidence","Internal audit report prepared by vendor staff","Reference call with an existing customer"],a:0,e:["Correct. A certificate issued by an independent accredited certification body is an objective third-party attestation.","Incorrect. A questionnaire is self-reported by the vendor. It is useful for screening but is not independent.","Incorrect. A reference call gives a customer's experience, which is valuable, but it is not an assessment of the security program.","Incorrect. An internal audit report is prepared by the vendor's own staff, so it lacks independence."]},
// ---------- 5.4 Compliance (16) ----------
{o:"5.4",q:"Each quarter, the compliance team presents the board of directors with the status of control testing and open findings. Which type of reporting is this?",c:["External compliance reporting","Regulatory examination by an agency","Third-party attestation report","Internal compliance reporting"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. External reporting goes to parties outside the organization such as regulators or the public.","Incorrect. A regulatory examination is carried out by a government agency's examiners, not presented by the compliance team.","Incorrect. Attestation is an assurance statement from an outside party. This is the company's own report to its board.","Correct. Reporting to management or the board within the organization is internal compliance reporting."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"A bank submits required capital and security compliance reports to its federal regulator on a set schedule. Which type of reporting is this?",c:["Third-party attestation report","External compliance reporting","Internal compliance reporting","Regulatory examination by an agency"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Attestation is an outside party's assurance statement. Here the bank itself is submitting required reports.","Correct. Reports sent to regulators, customers or the public are external compliance reporting.","Incorrect. Internal reporting stays within the organization, such as updates to the board.","Incorrect. A regulatory examination is a review conducted by regulator staff, often on-site. The bank here is filing its own reports."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"A retailer is found to have processed EU residents' personal data without a lawful basis. Which consequence is MOST likely?",c:["Loss of the ability to process card payments","Civil penalties tiered by level of negligence","Fines up to 4% of annual global revenue","Criminal liability for officers who certify reports"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Loss of card processing is a PCI DSS contractual consequence. It would fit a cardholder-data violation.","Incorrect. Tiered civil penalties based on culpability are associated with HIPAA violations involving health information.","Correct. GDPR allows administrative fines of up to 4% of worldwide annual revenue for serious violations, such as lacking a lawful basis.","Incorrect. Officer certification and criminal liability are associated with SOX financial reporting violations."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"A SaaS provider with customers on six continents must satisfy privacy laws from many jurisdictions at once. Which scope of legal implications does this represent?",c:["Global legal implications","National legal implications","Regional legal implications","Local legal implications"],a:0,e:["Correct. Operating across many countries and continents means obligations from multiple legal systems, which is a global scope.","Incorrect. National implications involve the law of a single country. The provider faces many countries.","Incorrect. Regional implications cover a bloc or state-level area, such as the EU alone. Customers here span six continents.","Incorrect. Local implications arise from city or county rules. They are narrower than the stem describes."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"A national law requires that citizens' personal data be stored on servers physically located inside the country. Which concept does this requirement describe?",c:["Data localization requirement","Data minimization requirement","Right-to-erasure requirement","Data retention requirement"],a:0,e:["Correct. Localization laws restrict where data may be stored or processed, creating national legal obligations for cloud design.","Incorrect. A retention schedule defines how long data is kept, not where.","Incorrect. The right to erasure lets individuals demand deletion of their data. It does not govern server location.","Incorrect. Minimization limits how much data is collected. It does not control storage location."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"A former customer emails a company asking it to delete all personal data it holds about her, because the data is no longer needed for the original purpose. Which privacy right is she exercising?",c:["Right of access to the data","Right to object to processing","Right to data portability","Right to erasure of the data"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. The right of access gives a copy of the data held. It would fit a customer asking what information the company has.","Incorrect. The right to object lets individuals contest certain processing, such as direct marketing. It does not demand deletion of all data.","Incorrect. Portability lets individuals receive their data in a reusable format to move elsewhere. It does not require deletion.","Correct. Under GDPR Article 17, individuals can request erasure in various circumstances, including when the data is no longer necessary."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"A customer emails a company asking for a copy of all personal data it holds about him. In privacy-law terms, what is the customer?",c:["Data custodian of the records","Data processor of the records","Data subject of the records","Data controller of the records"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A custodian is an internal technical role that protects the data. A customer outside the company is not one.","Incorrect. The processor acts for the controller, for example a hosting or payroll vendor. The customer does not.","Correct. The data subject is the identifiable individual the personal data relates to, and holds rights such as access.","Incorrect. The controller is the organization that determines why and how the data is processed, here the company."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"A company asks new customers to actively tick an unticked box to receive marketing emails that are not needed to deliver the service. Customers can withdraw at any time. Which GDPR lawful basis is being used?",c:["Contractual necessity for delivery","Consent of the data subject","Legal obligation of the company","Legitimate interest of the company"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Contractual necessity covers processing required to perform the contract. The marketing emails are not needed to deliver the service.","Correct. Consent must be freely given, specific and revocable, and an active opt-in with easy withdrawal matches that.","Incorrect. Legal obligation applies when law requires the processing, such as tax record keeping. No law requires marketing emails.","Incorrect. Legitimate interest relies on the organization's balanced interests and requires no opt-in. It would fit fraud prevention."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"A company selected its cloud vendor after investigating its security practices. It now continuously patches, monitors and trains staff as a prudent organization would. What is this ongoing responsibility called?",c:["Due process in disciplinary action","Due diligence in investigating","Due care in operating controls","Duty of confidentiality to clients"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Due process is a fairness concept in disciplinary or legal proceedings and does not address maintaining security controls.","Incorrect. Due diligence is the investigation that informs decisions, such as vetting the vendor. It is the earlier step.","Correct. Due care means taking reasonable, continuing steps to protect assets, as a prudent organization would.","Incorrect. A duty of confidentiality limits disclosure of information. It does not describe maintaining controls."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"Tools check server configurations against the CIS benchmark in real time and alert the compliance team as soon as a setting drifts. Which compliance activity is this?",c:["Third-party attestation report","Continuous compliance monitoring","Annual internal audit of controls","Self-assessment questionnaire"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Third-party attestation is an outside party's assurance, issued periodically.","Correct. Automated, real-time checking with drift alerts is continuous compliance monitoring.","Incorrect. An annual audit is a point-in-time review once a year and would not alert as soon as drift occurs.","Incorrect. A self-assessment questionnaire is completed manually at intervals and lacks real-time detection."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"Every year, each employee signs a statement confirming they read and understood the security policy. Which compliance activity is this?",c:["Internal control audit of policy","Phishing simulation campaign","Compliance automation scan","Signed policy acknowledgment"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. An internal audit tests whether controls operate as designed. It does not collect staff acknowledgments.","Incorrect. A phishing simulation tests behavior rather than confirming that people read the policy.","Incorrect. A compliance scan checks system configurations automatically. It does not involve staff signing a statement.","Correct. Signed acknowledgments provide documented evidence that staff are aware of policy requirements."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"A newsletter signup form is redesigned to ask only for an email address, removing fields for birth date and phone number that serve no stated purpose. Which privacy principle is being applied?",c:["Data minimization principle","Purpose limitation principle","Storage limitation principle","Data sovereignty principle"],a:0,e:["Correct. Data minimization means collecting only the personal data necessary for the stated purpose.","Incorrect. Purpose limitation restricts using data beyond its stated purposes. The change here reduces what is collected in the first place.","Incorrect. Storage limitation concerns how long data is kept. The change affects what is collected, not how long it is retained.","Incorrect. Data sovereignty concerns which country's laws apply to data based on where it is stored."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"A company keeps a catalog of what personal data it holds, where it is stored and why it is processed. Why does this matter for privacy compliance?",c:["It supports data subject requests and records of processing","It identifies vulnerabilities in the stored data sets","It documents network paths so firewall rules can be reviewed","It establishes recovery time objectives for each critical system"],a:0,e:["Correct. A data inventory lets the company locate data to answer access and erasure requests and to maintain required records of processing.","Incorrect. Vulnerabilities are found by scanning and testing. An inventory records what is held and why.","Incorrect. Network paths for firewall reviews come from network diagrams and flow documentation.","Incorrect. Recovery objectives come from a business impact analysis, not a personal data inventory."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"A broker-dealer repeatedly fails regulatory examinations and the regulator bars it from operating in the state. Which consequence of non-compliance is this?",c:["Contractual impacts with partners","Monetary fines assessed per violation","Loss of license to operate","Reputational damage with customers"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Contractual impacts include partner penalties or termination. The stem describes a regulator's action.","Incorrect. Fines are monetary penalties and may accompany exam failures, but barring the firm from operating is a license consequence.","Correct. Regulators can revoke or suspend licenses, ending permission to conduct a regulated activity.","Incorrect. Reputational damage is a consequence of public perception, not of a regulator's formal bar."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"A hospital decides why and how patient data is collected and hires a cloud email vendor to store and send it under a contract and the hospital's instructions. Which relationship describes this under GDPR terms?",c:["Hospital is controller; vendor is joint controller","Hospital is processor; vendor is controller","Hospital is owner; vendor is custodian","Hospital is controller; vendor is processor"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A joint controller also determines purposes and means. The vendor here acts only on the hospital's instructions.","Incorrect. This reverses the roles. The vendor takes instructions and does not decide purposes.","Incorrect. Owner and custodian are internal governance roles, not the GDPR terms for two separate organizations.","Correct. The party deciding purposes and means is the controller. The vendor acting on its instructions is the processor."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"A US-only merchant suffers a breach and is found non-compliant with PCI DSS. Which outcome is MOST likely?",c:["Enforcement over financial reporting by the SEC","Penalties from the acquirer and card brands","Civil penalties from the HHS Office for Civil Rights","Administrative fines from an EU supervisory authority"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. The SEC enforces securities and financial reporting laws such as SOX, not card data standards.","Correct. PCI DSS is enforced contractually, so penalties come through the acquiring bank and card brands, including possible loss of card processing.","Incorrect. HHS OCR enforces HIPAA, which covers health information. It would fit a breach of protected health information.","Incorrect. EU authorities enforce GDPR. The stem says the merchant is US-only, so GDPR would not usually apply."]},
// ---------- 5.5 Audits and assessments (16) ----------
{o:"5.5",q:"A penetration tester is given network diagrams, application source code and administrator credentials before the engagement begins. Which type of test is this?",c:["Black box (unknown environment) test","Physical penetration test of facility","White box (known environment) test","Gray box (partially known) test"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Black box testers start with no information and mimic an outside attacker. Diagrams and source code rule that out.","Incorrect. A physical test targets locks, guards and entry controls. The materials here relate to a technical test.","Correct. Full prior knowledge of the environment is a white box or known environment test, which allows the deepest coverage.","Incorrect. Gray box testers receive only partial information, such as a standard account. Full source code and admin credentials exceed that."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"A tester receives only the company's name and must discover everything else, as a real external attacker would. Which type of test is this?",c:["Physical penetration test of facility","Black box (unknown environment) test","White box (known environment) test","Gray box (partially known) test"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A physical test targets facility controls. The stem describes discovery of a network environment.","Correct. With no prior knowledge, the tester must perform discovery from scratch, simulating an outside attacker.","Incorrect. White box testing begins with full knowledge of diagrams and code.","Incorrect. Gray box testing begins with partial information, such as an account or IP ranges. Only a name is given here."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"A tester is given a standard user account and a list of IP ranges but no architecture documentation. Which type of test is this?",c:["Gray box (partially known) test","Black box (unknown environment) test","White box (known environment) test","Physical penetration test of facility"],a:0,e:["Correct. Partial information, such as a low-privilege account and address ranges, defines a gray box test.","Incorrect. Black box provides no information beyond the target's identity. The tester here has an account and ranges.","Incorrect. White box testing provides complete information including documentation and often source code.","Incorrect. A physical test examines facility access controls. This engagement is technical."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"At the start of an engagement, a tester reads the target's job postings, LinkedIn profiles and public DNS records without sending any traffic to its systems. Which phase is this?",c:["Active reconnaissance phase","Post-exploitation lateral movement","Credentialed scanning phase","Passive reconnaissance phase"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Active reconnaissance sends probes to the target, such as port scans, and can be detected.","Incorrect. Lateral movement happens after a foothold is gained inside the network.","Incorrect. Credentialed scanning logs in to systems with provided accounts. No systems are touched here.","Correct. Gathering information from public sources without interacting with the target is passive reconnaissance (OSINT)."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"A tester runs Nmap against the target's public IP ranges to find open ports and services. Which phase is this?",c:["Reporting and documentation phase","Exploitation of discovered flaws","Passive reconnaissance phase","Active reconnaissance phase"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Reporting documents findings at the end of the engagement.","Incorrect. Exploitation attempts to take advantage of a discovered weakness. Port scanning only identifies what is exposed.","Incorrect. Passive reconnaissance uses public sources without contacting the target.","Correct. Scanning sends packets to the target, which is active reconnaissance and may trigger detection."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"After an internal review of controls against policy, a senior officer signs a formal statement that the controls are in place and operating effectively. What is this statement?",c:["Management attestation of controls","Vulnerability scan results report","Regulatory examination finding","Penetration test findings report"],a:0,e:["Correct. An attestation is a formal declaration that controls exist and are effective, often signed by management or an auditor.","Incorrect. A scan report lists vulnerabilities detected by automated tools. It is not a signed assurance.","Incorrect. A regulatory examination finding is the regulator's conclusion, not management's signed statement.","Incorrect. A penetration test report documents exploitable weaknesses found during testing, not a statement of effectiveness."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"A government agency sends examiners on site to a bank to verify compliance with financial regulations. What is this activity?",c:["Internal audit by in-house staff","Regulatory examination","Independent third-party audit","Self-assessment by control owners"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Internal audit is performed by the organization's own auditors, not by a government agency.","Correct. Regulators examine regulated entities to verify compliance with legal requirements.","Incorrect. An independent third-party audit is commissioned from an outside firm, such as a CPA firm, not performed by a regulator.","Incorrect. A self-assessment is conducted by the organization on itself."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"An independent CPA firm audits a SaaS provider's controls over 12 months and issues a report that customers receive under NDA. What is this activity?",c:["Self-assessment by control owners","Internal audit by in-house staff","Independent third-party audit","Regulatory examination"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A self-assessment is performed by the organization itself and is not independent.","Incorrect. An internal audit uses in-house auditors and would not carry the independence customers expect.","Correct. An external, independent audit such as SOC 2 Type II provides objective assurance that can be shared with customers.","Incorrect. A regulatory exam is conducted by a government agency, not a commissioned CPA firm."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"Which body within an organization provides independent oversight of the internal audit function and internal controls, reporting directly to the board of directors?",c:["Security steering committee of management","Audit committee of the board","Incident response team for events","Change advisory board for changes"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A steering committee is made up of management and guides security priorities. It lacks the independence of an audit committee.","Correct. The audit committee oversees internal audit and controls independently of management, reporting to the board.","Incorrect. An incident response team handles security events when they occur.","Incorrect. A change advisory board reviews and approves technical changes."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"Before an official audit, a company's security team compares its own controls to the NIST Cybersecurity Framework to find gaps. What is this activity?",c:["Bug bounty program with researchers","Third-party audit by an outside firm","Internal self-assessment by staff","Regulatory examination by a regulator"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A bug bounty invites outside researchers to report vulnerabilities for rewards. It is not a framework gap review.","Incorrect. A third-party audit is performed by an outside firm. Here the company's own staff do the work.","Correct. Evaluating one's own controls to find gaps in advance is an internal self-assessment.","Incorrect. A regulatory exam is conducted by a government agency."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"A team is hired to emulate a specific APT group's tactics against the company, without telling defenders, to test detection and response. Which type of engagement is this?",c:["Offensive (red team) engagement","Physical penetration test of facility","Defensive (blue team) exercise","Integrated (purple team) exercise"],a:0,e:["Correct. Red teams emulate real adversaries, typically without warning defenders, to test detection and response.","Incorrect. A physical test targets facility entry controls. The stem emphasizes emulating a threat group's tactics and testing detection.","Incorrect. The blue team defends and responds. In this scenario they are the ones being tested.","Incorrect. Purple teaming has attackers and defenders collaborate openly. The stem says defenders are not told."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"Attackers and defenders work together during an exercise, sharing techniques and findings in real time so detections can be tuned immediately. Which type of exercise is this?",c:["Physical penetration test of facility","Offensive (red team) engagement","Defensive (blue team) exercise","Integrated (purple team) exercise"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A physical test targets entry controls such as locks and guards. The stem describes tuning detections.","Incorrect. Red team engagements typically run covertly, and findings are reported at the end.","Incorrect. A blue team exercise focuses on defenders detecting and responding. It does not include real-time collaboration with attackers.","Correct. Purple teaming integrates offense and defense so that findings immediately improve detections."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"A tester follows employees through badge-controlled doors into the data center and plants a device on a network jack. Which type of test is this?",c:["Wireless penetration test","Phishing simulation campaign","Web application penetration test","Physical penetration test"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A wireless test examines Wi-Fi security. The tester here is entering the facility.","Incorrect. A phishing simulation uses deceptive messages to test users. This engagement uses physical entry.","Incorrect. A web application test examines flaws in web software, not building access.","Correct. Testing whether someone can gain physical access and plant devices evaluates physical controls such as locks, guards and vestibules."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"At the end of a penetration test, what should the client expect to receive as the main deliverable?",c:["A report of findings, evidence and remediation advice","A signed statement that no exploitable weaknesses remain","Systems patched by the testers during the engagement","Raw scanner output listing the hosts and open ports found"],a:0,e:["Correct. The report documents findings, evidence and recommendations. Remediation is then planned by the client.","Incorrect. Testers do not usually patch systems. Remediation is the client's responsibility, and a separate engagement would be needed.","Incorrect. A penetration test cannot prove the absence of weaknesses. It reports those found within its scope.","Incorrect. Raw tool output has little value without analysis. Testers deliver interpreted findings."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"A firm is about to run an active penetration test against a client's production network. Which item makes the test legally authorized?",c:["Verbal approval from the IT operations manager","Signed written authorization from the owner","Cyber insurance policy covering the testing team","NDA signed by the testing firm"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Verbal approval from a manager is not documented and may not come from someone with authority.","Correct. Signed authorization from someone with the authority to approve defines the scope and makes the test lawful.","Incorrect. Insurance can cover losses from mistakes, but it does not authorize access to systems.","Incorrect. An NDA covers confidentiality but does not grant permission to test."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"The board wants assurance on the company's controls that is independent of management and can be shared with customers. Which type of review best meets this need?",c:["Management review by senior executives","Internal audit by an in-house team","External audit by an independent firm","Self-assessment performed by control owners"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Self-assessments are performed by the control owners themselves and are not independent.","Incorrect. Management review is an internal oversight activity and lacks independence from management.","Correct. An external audit provides independence from management and results that customers can rely on.","Incorrect. Internal audit is useful, but its staff are part of the organization, so customers give it less weight."]},
// ---------- 5.6 Security awareness (16) ----------
{o:"5.6",q:"The security team sends simulated phishing emails to staff and enrolls anyone who clicks in a short training module right away. Which awareness activity is this?",c:["Simulated phishing with immediate training","Anonymous tip hotline for reporting concerns","Vulnerability scan of the mail gateway","Tabletop exercise for the executive team"],a:0,e:["Correct. Simulated phishing measures behavior, and immediate training reinforces the lesson at the moment it is most relevant.","Incorrect. An anonymous reporting program lets staff raise concerns. It does not send test emails or train people who click.","Incorrect. A vulnerability scan examines servers for weaknesses and does not train people.","Incorrect. A tabletop is a discussion-based exercise for walking through a scenario, not a test of individual users."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"Which habit is the BEST way for an employee to recognize a phishing attempt?",c:["Reply to the sender to ask if the message is genuine","Open attachments in the preview pane first","Trust messages showing the company logo and signature","Check the sender's real address and hover over links"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Replying confirms that the mailbox is active and may reach the attacker. Verify using a known channel instead.","Incorrect. A preview pane can still render malicious content. Previewing does not help in deciding if a message is legitimate.","Incorrect. Logos and signatures are easy to copy, so they prove nothing about authenticity.","Correct. Inspecting the actual address and link destination exposes spoofing and mismatches before any action is taken."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"A project manager shares release plans only with people who need them and avoids discussing details in public channels. Which security awareness concept is this?",c:["Social engineering awareness training","Password hygiene and rotation","Operational security (OPSEC)","Data loss prevention (DLP)"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Social engineering awareness teaches staff to recognize manipulation by attackers. It does not describe limiting information sharing.","Incorrect. Password hygiene concerns how credentials are created and protected.","Correct. OPSEC means protecting sensitive information through careful behavior, such as limiting who knows what.","Incorrect. DLP is a technical control that detects and blocks data leaving the organization. The manager is using judgment, not a tool."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"An employee notices a colleague downloading large volumes of confidential files late at night, which is unusual for that role. What should awareness training tell the employee to do?",c:["Confront the colleague directly about the downloads","Report it to security through the proper channel","Wait for the next performance review to mention it","Delete the files from the share to stop the loss"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Confronting a possible insider can tip them off and destroy evidence.","Correct. Training should teach staff to recognize anomalous behavior and report it so security can investigate.","Incorrect. A review may be months away. Possible exfiltration needs prompt reporting.","Incorrect. Deleting files alters evidence and is outside the employee's authority."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"Which training topic addresses employees accidentally causing incidents, such as sending files to the wrong recipient, with no malicious intent?",c:["Social engineering resistance","Operational security practices","Malicious insider threat indicators","Unintentional behavior prevention"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Social engineering resistance addresses manipulation by attackers, which is not involved when an employee simply makes a mistake.","Incorrect. OPSEC concerns deliberate limits on sharing sensitive information, not preventing accidental errors.","Incorrect. Malicious insider training covers deliberate harm. The stem says there is no malicious intent.","Correct. Awareness programs cover careless or unintentional mistakes, such as misaddressed email, as well as malicious acts."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"The security team tracks click rates and report rates from phishing simulations over time to judge whether training is working. Which activity is this?",c:["Policy attestation tracking","Incident report documentation","Reporting of awareness metrics","Vulnerability metrics dashboard"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Attestation tracking records who has acknowledged policies, not how people behave in simulations.","Incorrect. Incident reports document specific real events, not simulation outcomes.","Correct. Metrics such as click and report rates show whether training changes behavior, and trends guide adjustments.","Incorrect. A vulnerability dashboard tracks technical weaknesses in systems, not human behavior."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"A large share of the workforce now works from home. Which training content would help these employees MOST?",c:["Mantrap use, guard procedures and camera review","Home Wi-Fi hardening, VPN use and screen locking","Badge use, visitor escorts and clean desk checks","Server room access, rack locks and fire suppression"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Mantraps, guards and cameras are facility controls that remote staff do not interact with.","Correct. Remote staff face risks on home networks and in shared spaces, so training should cover Wi-Fi, VPN and device locking.","Incorrect. Badge use, escorts and clean desk checks apply to on-site offices.","Incorrect. Server room and rack security matter to data center staff, not to home workers."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"Every month, management receives a security awareness summary of trends and completion rates, produced on a fixed schedule regardless of events. What type of report is this?",c:["Recurring awareness report","Ad hoc post-incident report","Initial baseline assessment","Executive risk acceptance memo"],a:0,e:["Correct. A report produced on a regular schedule, regardless of events, is a recurring report.","Incorrect. An incident report documents a specific event when it occurs, not a fixed schedule.","Incorrect. A baseline assessment is a one-time measurement used as a starting point.","Incorrect. A risk acceptance memo documents a decision to accept a specific risk, not routine status."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"The security team wants employees to report suspicious emails more often. Which approach is MOST likely to increase reporting?",c:["A one-click report button with no-blame follow-up","Send reports to a shared mailbox reviewed quarterly","Publish the names of employees who click simulations","Require reports to be filed through the HR portal form"],a:0,e:["Correct. Easy reporting and a blame-free response encourage employees to speak up quickly.","Incorrect. A cumbersome process through an unrelated team creates friction, so fewer reports are made.","Incorrect. Quarterly review means reports go unanswered, which discourages staff and delays response.","Incorrect. Public shaming discourages staff from reporting and from admitting mistakes."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"An audit finds employees reuse the same password for work and personal accounts and share it over chat. Which training topic should be strengthened?",c:["Clean desk and physical security","Social engineering resistance","Anomalous behavior recognition","Password management practices"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Clean desk training covers leaving sensitive material exposed in the workspace.","Incorrect. Social engineering training teaches resistance to manipulation. The audit found poor credential handling.","Incorrect. Anomalous behavior recognition teaches staff to spot and report suspicious activity by others.","Correct. Reuse and sharing of passwords show a gap in password management training, such as unique passphrases and password managers."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"New hires must complete security training before they receive credentials to any system. At which point in the employee lifecycle does this occur?",c:["Offboarding stage of the lifecycle","Role-change stage of the lifecycle","Onboarding stage of the lifecycle","Annual refresher stage of the lifecycle"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Offboarding covers departure, such as revoking access and returning assets.","Incorrect. Role-change training happens when an existing employee moves to a new position with different access.","Correct. Training before first access happens during onboarding and sets expectations early.","Incorrect. Annual refreshers repeat training for current staff. New hires have not yet had initial training."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"Which policy governs what employees may post about their work, projects and employer on their personal accounts?",c:["Acceptable use policy for staff","Social media policy for staff","Clean desk policy for staff","Bring-your-own-device policy"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. An AUP governs use of company IT resources. It would fit rules on using work laptops for personal browsing.","Correct. A social media policy limits oversharing about the company that could help attackers or leak information.","Incorrect. A clean desk policy requires sensitive materials to be secured when workspaces are unattended.","Incorrect. A BYOD policy covers using personal devices to access company data, not what people post online."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"An employee finds an unlabeled USB drive in the lobby. What should awareness training tell the employee to do?",c:["Give it to the security team without connecting it","Connect it to a guest-network laptop to find the owner","Wipe it on a personal computer and keep it","Leave it in place and tell reception"],a:0,e:["Correct. Unknown media may be a baiting attack, so it should go to security unconnected for safe handling.","Incorrect. Connecting it to any computer risks malware executing, even on a guest network.","Incorrect. Connecting it to a personal computer risks infection and also keeps company-related media away from security.","Incorrect. Leaving it lets another person pick it up and plug it in, and security would not know about the possible attack."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"Which behavior helps prevent tailgating into a badge-controlled area?",c:["Hold the door for colleagues carrying boxes","Direct unbadged people to reception","Prop the door during the morning rush","Lend a spare badge to lost visitors"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Holding the door lets others enter without badging, even if they look like colleagues.","Correct. Politely redirecting people without badges keeps access limited to authorized individuals.","Incorrect. Propped doors bypass access control and let anyone enter.","Incorrect. Lending badges breaks accountability and gives access to people who were never authorized."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"What should awareness training teach employees about handling signs of a possible insider threat, such as unusual access and repeated policy violations?",c:["Collect evidence personally on the coworker before telling anyone","Check the coworker's personal accounts to confirm suspicions","Report observed indicators through the proper channel","Wait until management announces a suspect to staff"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Collecting evidence on your own can compromise an investigation and may break policy or law.","Incorrect. Checking personal accounts can violate privacy rules and is not the employee's role.","Correct. Staff should recognize indicators and report them promptly so trained personnel can investigate.","Incorrect. Waiting delays response. Staff should report concerns instead of waiting for official notice."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"A company is building a security awareness program from scratch. Before choosing any content or tools, which activity should come FIRST?",c:["Set up dashboards to report metrics to executives","Launch a company-wide baseline phishing simulation","Select a vendor's security course library","Assess audience needs and organizational risks"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Metrics reporting requires a program and defined goals first.","Incorrect. A baseline simulation is a useful measurement but should follow needs assessment and planning.","Incorrect. Choosing a library before understanding needs risks buying content that does not fit. This comes after assessment.","Correct. Content should be built around the organization's actual risks and the needs of different audiences, so assessment comes first."]}
]);
